WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:08.090
This is week 2 of the
systems lecture, where

00:00:08.090 --> 00:00:10.700
we try to leverage the most
out of the hardware we have

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:13.430
to make models train faster.

00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:18.870
And last week, we talked about
parallelism within a single GPU,

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:21.500
and this week, we're
talking about parallelism

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:23.250
across multiple GPUs.

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:25.830
So this is a picture you
should have in your head.

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:27.840
So we have a bunch of nodes.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:31.490
These are basically
computers that each

00:00:31.490 --> 00:00:32.840
have a number of GPUs--

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:33.810
usually 8.

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:36.320
And within each
GPU, there's a bunch

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:42.240
of streaming multiprocessors, or
SMs, which actually do the work.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:46.010
And you see that in green here
are essentially the memory

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:47.150
and the communications.

00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:51.860
Within each SM, you have
a very small L1 cache.

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:55.860
On a GPU, you have a
high-bandwidth memory, HBM,

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:56.850
which is bigger.

00:00:56.850 --> 00:01:01.560
And then you have these links
that connect the different GPUs.

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:06.330
So the way to think
about it is that compute

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:09.850
has to happen within
the SM on these ALUs.

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:14.010
And a compute needs inputs
and needs to write outputs.

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:19.110
And generally, the inputs and
outputs can be relatively far.

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:20.850
If you're lucky,
they're on the L1 cache.

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:24.130
If you're slightly less
unlucky, they're in HBM.

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:27.960
And now this week, we're talking
about multi-GPU and multi-node

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:30.090
training where the data
that you might need

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:32.370
might be across on another GPU.

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:34.560
So the name of the
game is, how do you

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:38.640
structure all your computation
to avoid data transfer

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:39.640
bottlenecks?

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:43.850
Because we want to, remember,
keep the arithmetic intensity

00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:44.350
high.

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:48.460
We want to saturate our
GPUs, make them hum along.

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:51.520
And generally, data transfer
is going to be a lot slower,

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:54.600
so we have to-- that's
going to be the bottleneck.

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:57.390
So last week we saw a bunch
of different techniques

00:01:57.390 --> 00:02:01.440
to try to do that within a GPU,
including fusion and tiling.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:05.030
So the idea basically is that
instead of reading and writing

00:02:05.030 --> 00:02:08.000
from HBM, you can
load into L1 cache,

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:10.699
or I guess a shared
memory, which

00:02:10.699 --> 00:02:12.410
is using the same type of--

00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:17.990
has the same speed,
and just work there

00:02:17.990 --> 00:02:20.390
on your local scratchpad
and then write out

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:23.720
to HBM only judiciously.

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:25.670
And this week, we
started looking

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:28.610
at the communication
across GPUs and nodes

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:32.420
where we have to replicate and
shard our models and parameters

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:35.930
and optimize our
states, and there,

00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:40.190
the way we do that will
determine the cost.

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:42.283
So here's a--

00:02:42.283 --> 00:02:43.700
I'm taking a little
bit of liberty

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:46.080
to put everything
in one hierarchy,

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:49.440
you can think, from
small/fast to big/slow.

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:53.370
So the smallest and fastest is
on a single node, single GPU,

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:57.790
you have L1 cache that's
extremely fast, but very small.

00:02:57.790 --> 00:03:01.690
And then you have
HBM on a single GPU.

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:08.830
And then between GPUs on the
same node, we have NVLink,

00:03:08.830 --> 00:03:11.470
and then finally,
we have NVSwitch.

00:03:11.470 --> 00:03:15.840
And of course, this is all
in the NVIDIA ecosystem.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:22.230
So the idea is that many of the
core concepts of minimizing data

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:26.370
transfer are really the
same, but now the mechanics

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:30.000
are a bit different because
L1 behaves differently

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:33.900
than these NV switches.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:36.390
So this lecture is
going to be mostly

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:40.858
about concretizing the concepts
from the lecture in code.

00:03:40.858 --> 00:03:42.400
There's going to be
a few new things,

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:45.120
but Tatsu did an excellent
job of giving you

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:48.010
an overview of all the
different types of parallelism.

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:49.810
I'm going to try to
anchor it in the code

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:53.610
so we can more deeply
understand what's going on.

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:56.830
And then we're going to have--

00:03:56.830 --> 00:03:59.410
I'm going to refer to
this standard out file

00:03:59.410 --> 00:04:02.680
here, which is the output
of running this lecture.

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:05.380
There were some minor
issues I'll spare you

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:08.650
of where if you have
multiprocessing,

00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:12.100
then this framework
doesn't quite work.

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:15.250
So this lecture has two parts.

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:19.029
In part 1, we're going to
look at the building blocks--

00:04:19.029 --> 00:04:21.610
collective operations, which
we discussed last time,

00:04:21.610 --> 00:04:24.320
how this is implemented
in NCCL and PyTorch,

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:26.660
and then we're going to
do some benchmarking.

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:29.590
And then in part 2, we're
going to look at actually

00:04:29.590 --> 00:04:31.720
distributed training,
data, tensor,

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:34.800
and pipeline parallelism.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:35.300
OK.

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:38.770
So let's start with
collective operations.

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:41.590
So collective operations
are these primitives

00:04:41.590 --> 00:04:44.930
that are used generally for
distributed programming,

00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:48.290
and collective means
that you have many nodes.

00:04:48.290 --> 00:04:51.010
These are actually
quite old, from at least

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:54.750
the '80s and the parallel
programming literature.

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:58.710
And generally, they provide
a better abstraction

00:04:58.710 --> 00:05:02.400
than trying to manage the
point-to-point communication

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:03.100
yourself.

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:07.950
So these are really
tried-and-true primitives that

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:10.090
have stood the test of time.

00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:12.070
So a bit of terminology.

00:05:12.070 --> 00:05:14.820
So world size refers to
essentially the number

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:15.420
of devices--

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:16.440
for example, 4.

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:22.500
And the rank, confusingly, if
you're used to linear algebra,

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:23.860
actually just refers to divide.

00:05:23.860 --> 00:05:26.400
So we have rank 0, rank
1, rank 2, and rank 3

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:28.290
if you have four devices.

00:05:28.290 --> 00:05:34.110
OK, so the collective
operations are as follows.

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:38.370
So starting from
broadcast, the idea

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:42.150
is you have t0 on
one of the ranks,

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:44.100
and you just want
to put it on all

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:46.750
the other ranks-- or all ranks.

00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:48.460
So that's very straightforward.

00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:52.910
Scatter is similar, but
you have four values,

00:05:52.910 --> 00:05:56.540
and you want to put each of
the values on different ranks.

00:05:56.540 --> 00:05:58.810
So each of the ranks get
different values, not

00:05:58.810 --> 00:06:01.990
the same value.

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:06.970
Gather is the inverse of scatter
where you have each rank having

00:06:06.970 --> 00:06:09.340
a different value, and then
you bring them all together

00:06:09.340 --> 00:06:12.370
onto one rank.

00:06:12.370 --> 00:06:15.730
Reduce is the same as
gather, except for instead

00:06:15.730 --> 00:06:19.000
of concatenating, you add them.

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:22.070
all_gather is the
same as gather,

00:06:22.070 --> 00:06:25.870
except for you just do it
for all the destinations.

00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:29.870
Gather was just
rank 0, or rank 1

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:32.950
or rank 2, or any individual
rank. all_gather as you do it

00:06:32.950 --> 00:06:34.900
for all of them.

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:36.700
And then finally,
reduce_scatter--

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:39.650
I couldn't find a
good picture of this,

00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:43.210
so I'm reusing the
one from last time,

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:50.780
is like reduce where you take
a bunch of different values

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:55.700
and you add them or perform
other commutative operation

00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:59.190
on them and put it on one rank.

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:03.620
But like scatter,
you're going to be

00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:07.250
putting different
pieces of the vector

00:07:07.250 --> 00:07:11.630
or tensor on different ranks.

00:07:11.630 --> 00:07:16.220
And remember that all_reduce
is equivalent to reduce

00:07:16.220 --> 00:07:19.700
plus all_gather.

00:07:19.700 --> 00:07:22.910
So the way to remember
this terminology

00:07:22.910 --> 00:07:27.150
is as follows because
it can get confusing

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:29.510
which one's all_gather,
which one's reduce_scatter,

00:07:29.510 --> 00:07:32.510
is that reduce just
means you're performing

00:07:32.510 --> 00:07:37.190
some associative and commutative
operation like sum or min or max

00:07:37.190 --> 00:07:38.900
or average.

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:42.410
Broadcast-scatter is
the inverse of gather.

00:07:42.410 --> 00:07:46.273
And all it just means, a
destination is all devices.

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:51.630
So hopefully this is a
review from last time.

00:07:54.820 --> 00:08:00.882
So, is there any questions
before I move on?

00:08:00.882 --> 00:08:02.840
Since we're going to
build on these primitives,

00:08:02.840 --> 00:08:05.600
so it's useful if
everyone understands.

00:08:12.942 --> 00:08:13.442
OK.

00:08:13.442 --> 00:08:18.280
So now let's see how this
is actually implemented

00:08:18.280 --> 00:08:20.830
in starting with the hardware.

00:08:20.830 --> 00:08:28.010
So here's classically what
hardware for GPUs looks like.

00:08:28.010 --> 00:08:31.160
So this is in the home.

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:34.030
You have a computer, I guess.

00:08:34.030 --> 00:08:35.990
And you have your CPUs.

00:08:35.990 --> 00:08:39.700
And generally,
you have your GPUs

00:08:39.700 --> 00:08:46.390
on one node that
communicate via PCI-E bus.

00:08:46.390 --> 00:08:49.790
And if you have
to go communicate

00:08:49.790 --> 00:08:53.090
between different
nodes, then this

00:08:53.090 --> 00:08:54.690
is all connected to ethernet.

00:08:54.690 --> 00:08:57.900
So this is typically
how machines were built.

00:08:57.900 --> 00:09:01.820
If you buy a GPU and you're
for gaming or something,

00:09:01.820 --> 00:09:05.690
this is probably what
your setup looks like.

00:09:05.690 --> 00:09:09.890
As we'll see, this is
suboptimal because there's

00:09:09.890 --> 00:09:10.970
a lot of overhead.

00:09:10.970 --> 00:09:14.940
When the data needs to get
shipped from GPU to GPU,

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:18.420
it has to go through the
kernel, get copied into buffers,

00:09:18.420 --> 00:09:22.680
and then go through this
transport over Ethernet,

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:26.030
and that introduces
a lot of overhead.

00:09:26.030 --> 00:09:29.030
So what has happened
in modern times

00:09:29.030 --> 00:09:31.910
with scientific computing
and deep learning

00:09:31.910 --> 00:09:34.430
is that if you know
that you're going

00:09:34.430 --> 00:09:37.670
to just string a bunch of
GPUs together and do something

00:09:37.670 --> 00:09:43.380
together, then we're just going
to hook the GPUs up directly,

00:09:43.380 --> 00:09:44.720
basically.

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:47.920
So in the NVIDIA
ecosystem, we have

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:51.820
NVLink that directly
connects the GPUs, therefore,

00:09:51.820 --> 00:09:53.330
bypassing the CPU--

00:09:53.330 --> 00:09:59.590
you don't need to go through
the kernel of the host machine.

00:09:59.590 --> 00:10:04.540
And even across nodes, we
can connect the GPUs directly

00:10:04.540 --> 00:10:06.080
via NVSwitch.

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:08.090
So therefore, we're
bypassing ethernet.

00:10:08.090 --> 00:10:11.080
And because ethernet was
developed a long time ago,

00:10:11.080 --> 00:10:15.350
clearly not for these
type of applications.

00:10:15.350 --> 00:10:18.760
So NVSwitch and NVLink
skip all of that

00:10:18.760 --> 00:10:22.060
and just optimize directly
for the type of workloads

00:10:22.060 --> 00:10:24.490
that we're interested in.

00:10:24.490 --> 00:10:30.400
So, if you look at
H100s, each node has--

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:37.310
or sorry, each GPU has 18
NVLinks generation 4 coming out.

00:10:37.310 --> 00:10:42.070
So that gives you a total
bandwidth of 900 gigabytes.

00:10:42.070 --> 00:10:46.010
If you compare to
these, it's certainly

00:10:46.010 --> 00:10:48.830
a lot faster than PCI-E,
and it's certainly way

00:10:48.830 --> 00:10:51.800
faster than ethernet.

00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:58.040
And in comparison, if you think
about the cost of just going

00:10:58.040 --> 00:11:02.960
from the SM to reading
from high-bandwidth memory,

00:11:02.960 --> 00:11:08.870
that's still quite a bit
faster by a factor of 4 or so.

00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:11.760
And of course, these numbers
are constantly changing.

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:16.700
With the new Blackwells, this
number is two or three times

00:11:16.700 --> 00:11:19.070
more, I believe.

00:11:19.070 --> 00:11:20.450
OK.

00:11:20.450 --> 00:11:21.350
Yeah?

00:11:21.350 --> 00:11:24.590
So the PC, [INAUDIBLE]
through the [INAUDIBLE] CPU

00:11:24.590 --> 00:11:26.330
and then another GPU?

00:11:26.330 --> 00:11:28.850
Or it's a direct link
between the GPUs?

00:11:28.850 --> 00:11:32.150
So the question
is for the PCI-E,

00:11:32.150 --> 00:11:35.430
how does the data
get transferred?

00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:38.995
I think it has to still
go through the CPU.

00:11:42.395 --> 00:11:43.520
Was there another question?

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:50.520
And the PCI-E was--

00:11:50.520 --> 00:11:52.680
I mean, it's
developed for things

00:11:52.680 --> 00:11:54.910
like-- other things are
connected to it as well,

00:11:54.910 --> 00:11:57.850
like your sound card or
your SSD or hard drive.

00:11:57.850 --> 00:12:02.550
So it's not really-- it's
a general purpose bus

00:12:02.550 --> 00:12:04.390
for communication of devices.

00:12:04.390 --> 00:12:05.850
Yeah?

00:12:05.850 --> 00:12:09.730
NVLink has a connection
with the CPU?

00:12:09.730 --> 00:12:10.230
Yeah.

00:12:10.230 --> 00:12:13.780
So the question is, NVLink
also connects to the CPU?

00:12:13.780 --> 00:12:16.320
We're going to see
a bit later how--

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:19.990
I think maybe just in this
slide, how things are connected.

00:12:19.990 --> 00:12:20.940
Yeah.

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:23.590
So you still need to talk
to your CPU, of course.

00:12:23.590 --> 00:12:25.410
Yeah.

00:12:25.410 --> 00:12:28.740
OK, so there's this
command you can run,

00:12:28.740 --> 00:12:32.100
and this produces
some output, which

00:12:32.100 --> 00:12:37.120
allows you to see how the
GPUs are actually connected.

00:12:37.120 --> 00:12:40.450
So I ran this on our cluster.

00:12:40.450 --> 00:12:42.930
There is 8 GPUs--

00:12:42.930 --> 00:12:45.230
I guess you won't be
able to get 8 GPUs,

00:12:45.230 --> 00:12:48.540
but I guess if you could, this
is what it would look like.

00:12:48.540 --> 00:12:51.470
And you see that between
every pair of GPUs,

00:12:51.470 --> 00:12:54.980
there's NV18 connecting.

00:12:54.980 --> 00:13:00.685
There's also these network
cards and other things.

00:13:04.340 --> 00:13:07.310
OK.

00:13:07.310 --> 00:13:07.890
Oh yeah.

00:13:07.890 --> 00:13:09.410
So then network
cards are basically

00:13:09.410 --> 00:13:13.180
what gives you the PCI-E
connection and the CPUs.

00:13:17.210 --> 00:13:19.560
OK, so that's the hardware.

00:13:19.560 --> 00:13:21.390
So how do you use the hardware?

00:13:21.390 --> 00:13:25.970
So NVIDIA has spent a lot
of time developing really

00:13:25.970 --> 00:13:30.650
good software on top of their,
I guess, really good hardware.

00:13:30.650 --> 00:13:33.710
And there's a collective
communication library

00:13:33.710 --> 00:13:35.780
by NVIDIA called NCCL.

00:13:35.780 --> 00:13:39.350
And this essentially translates
the collective operations,

00:13:39.350 --> 00:13:42.120
which we looked at
before, like all_reduce,

00:13:42.120 --> 00:13:45.490
into low-level packets that
need to be sent between GPUs.

00:13:45.490 --> 00:13:48.300
So this library actually
does a lot of work

00:13:48.300 --> 00:13:51.900
because it allows the programmer
just to operate that level of,

00:13:51.900 --> 00:13:54.870
I need this tensor to
appear on all the machines,

00:13:54.870 --> 00:13:55.880
and it just happens.

00:13:59.760 --> 00:14:02.400
So just a little
bit of what happens

00:14:02.400 --> 00:14:07.380
is when you configure,
set up NCCL, you bring up

00:14:07.380 --> 00:14:10.170
a bunch of devices.

00:14:10.170 --> 00:14:12.390
And there's some
communication that

00:14:12.390 --> 00:14:14.920
happens to figure out the
topology of the hardware.

00:14:14.920 --> 00:14:17.380
It optimizes the path
between the GPUs.

00:14:17.380 --> 00:14:21.630
And then when you actually call
these collective communication

00:14:21.630 --> 00:14:24.690
operations, it then launches
CUDA kernels to send and receive

00:14:24.690 --> 00:14:26.190
data.

00:14:26.190 --> 00:14:27.600
OK, so that's the NCCL.

00:14:27.600 --> 00:14:29.650
That's provided as a library.

00:14:29.650 --> 00:14:33.570
But NCCL is still a
bit too low level to us

00:14:33.570 --> 00:14:37.450
because most of what
we're doing is in Python.

00:14:37.450 --> 00:14:40.930
So PyTorch has this
torch.distributed library

00:14:40.930 --> 00:14:43.690
which essentially
provides a clean interface

00:14:43.690 --> 00:14:45.860
for these collective operations.

00:14:45.860 --> 00:14:50.390
Now from the comfort of
your PyTorch program,

00:14:50.390 --> 00:14:53.740
you can just write all_gather
into tensor on a tensor

00:14:53.740 --> 00:14:56.625
and it will appear on
all the different ranks.

00:14:59.170 --> 00:15:02.620
It also has this
nice useful feature

00:15:02.620 --> 00:15:06.050
that it supports multiple
backends for different hardware.

00:15:06.050 --> 00:15:08.630
So in particular, NCCL,
remember, was for GPU,

00:15:08.630 --> 00:15:12.220
but you can also run collective
operations-- remember,

00:15:12.220 --> 00:15:17.030
this is not GPU-specific, it's
just for any set of devices.

00:15:17.030 --> 00:15:21.550
So you can also do it for CPU
using this backend called gloo.

00:15:21.550 --> 00:15:24.980
So if you're debugging stuff on
your laptop for your assignment,

00:15:24.980 --> 00:15:27.610
for example, you can
use gloo and still

00:15:27.610 --> 00:15:32.290
be able to run things
without even a GPU.

00:15:32.290 --> 00:15:34.900
So anyway, that's another
advantage of having these

00:15:34.900 --> 00:15:37.590
high-level primitives, is that
they're much more portable than

00:15:37.590 --> 00:15:40.494
having to--

00:15:40.494 --> 00:15:43.350
only having something
that's very GPU-specific.

00:15:45.960 --> 00:15:48.237
Of course, the performance
is going to really depend

00:15:48.237 --> 00:15:50.070
on the hardware, but
at least logically, you

00:15:50.070 --> 00:15:51.823
can make sure your code runs.

00:15:54.510 --> 00:15:57.300
Distributed also supports
other high-level things

00:15:57.300 --> 00:16:00.850
like FSDP, which Tatsu
talked about last lecture,

00:16:00.850 --> 00:16:02.820
but we're not going
to use in this class

00:16:02.820 --> 00:16:06.070
because in the spirit of
developing things from scratch,

00:16:06.070 --> 00:16:09.000
that's just what
we're going to do.

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:12.300
OK, so let's look
at some examples

00:16:12.300 --> 00:16:15.730
of how torch.distributed
collective operations work.

00:16:15.730 --> 00:16:19.240
So there's this utility
function I wrote,

00:16:19.240 --> 00:16:21.670
which you can take a look at
it in the code if you want,

00:16:21.670 --> 00:16:26.650
which takes a function
and just runs this--

00:16:26.650 --> 00:16:30.390
basically, it's a wrapper
around Python multiprocessing

00:16:30.390 --> 00:16:34.680
where it just runs
four processes that

00:16:34.680 --> 00:16:36.070
execute this function.

00:16:36.070 --> 00:16:38.030
So when you're in
this function, you

00:16:38.030 --> 00:16:40.580
should think about it
as there's actually

00:16:40.580 --> 00:16:45.500
world_size number of processes
running this identical function

00:16:45.500 --> 00:16:48.920
where the rank indexes
from 0, 1, all the way

00:16:48.920 --> 00:16:50.840
to world_size minus 1.

00:16:50.840 --> 00:16:55.670
So right now, I'm stepping
through just one of the ranks

00:16:55.670 --> 00:16:59.750
because lectures
are not parallel.

00:16:59.750 --> 00:17:03.410
And so generally
what you do is you--

00:17:03.410 --> 00:17:08.160
the first thing, the process
needs to initialize itself.

00:17:08.160 --> 00:17:12.480
And you essentially-- they
need to find each other

00:17:12.480 --> 00:17:15.380
because you're a
multi-processor,

00:17:15.380 --> 00:17:16.920
you're running a
lot of processes,

00:17:16.920 --> 00:17:22.970
they need to connect to a single
host so that they can figure--

00:17:22.970 --> 00:17:25.579
know that each other exist.

00:17:25.579 --> 00:17:29.010
So note that this is not
where all of the data goes.

00:17:29.010 --> 00:17:32.870
The data goes through, NCCL, but
this is just for coordination.

00:17:32.870 --> 00:17:37.230
And since we a GPU,
we can use NCCL,

00:17:37.230 --> 00:17:40.460
otherwise you would use gloo.

00:17:40.460 --> 00:17:43.250
OK, so after you set up--

00:17:43.250 --> 00:17:45.000
so now we're going
to do some stuff.

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:50.060
There's this useful function
called barrier which basically

00:17:50.060 --> 00:17:53.030
waits for all the processes
in your process group

00:17:53.030 --> 00:17:55.260
to get to this point.

00:17:55.260 --> 00:17:58.580
Remember, everything is
running asynchronously.

00:17:58.580 --> 00:18:01.950
And in some cases, you just want
to have a synchronization point,

00:18:01.950 --> 00:18:03.860
so barrier does that.

00:18:03.860 --> 00:18:07.860
The reason I put it here is
actually for trivial reasons

00:18:07.860 --> 00:18:11.180
because I want all these
print statements to be grouped

00:18:11.180 --> 00:18:13.910
together, but
there's other reasons

00:18:13.910 --> 00:18:16.920
why you might want to use
barrier as we'll get to later.

00:18:16.920 --> 00:18:20.990
So I'm going to, for
each of these groups,

00:18:20.990 --> 00:18:22.140
construct a tensor.

00:18:22.140 --> 00:18:27.710
So the tensor is 0,
1, 2, 3 plus the rank.

00:18:27.710 --> 00:18:31.460
So I'm going to print
out for each rank,

00:18:31.460 --> 00:18:34.280
before the all_reduce,
what does it look like?

00:18:34.280 --> 00:18:38.650
OK, so here's what
it looks like.

00:18:38.650 --> 00:18:41.200
Can people read
that in the back?

00:18:41.200 --> 00:18:41.830
Yes?

00:18:41.830 --> 00:18:43.300
OK, good.

00:18:43.300 --> 00:18:46.780
All right, so on rank
0, it's 0, 1, 2, 3.

00:18:46.780 --> 00:18:48.680
For rank 1, 1, 2,
3, 4, and so on.

00:18:48.680 --> 00:18:51.670
And notice that because it's
async, the order is-- it's

00:18:51.670 --> 00:18:56.120
just out of order in whatever
order it happens to print.

00:18:56.120 --> 00:19:01.820
So each rank has a
different tensor.

00:19:01.820 --> 00:19:04.640
And then you all_reduce.

00:19:04.640 --> 00:19:07.210
So all_reduce, you
pass in that tensor.

00:19:07.210 --> 00:19:09.490
You say I want to sum it.

00:19:09.490 --> 00:19:11.330
In this case, I'm not
going to do async,

00:19:11.330 --> 00:19:15.550
but you can do async which
is useful for overlapping

00:19:15.550 --> 00:19:19.420
communication and computation.

00:19:19.420 --> 00:19:23.980
And then afterwards, what
happens after all_reduce,

00:19:23.980 --> 00:19:27.430
as advertised, basically
for the first component,

00:19:27.430 --> 00:19:29.230
you add them up, you get 6.

00:19:29.230 --> 00:19:32.660
This, you get 10, 14, and 18.

00:19:32.660 --> 00:19:37.910
So after all_reduce,
basically, this tensor

00:19:37.910 --> 00:19:44.540
gets overwritten with
the corresponding sum.

00:19:44.540 --> 00:19:49.490
So it's very, very
nice and simple to use.

00:19:49.490 --> 00:19:51.140
OK.

00:19:51.140 --> 00:19:54.800
So, let's do reduce_scatter.

00:19:54.800 --> 00:19:59.600
So reduce_scatter,
I'm going to create

00:19:59.600 --> 00:20:06.650
an input, which has dimension
world_size, in which case,

00:20:06.650 --> 00:20:08.210
this is 4.

00:20:08.210 --> 00:20:12.590
And I'm going to allocate an
output because reduce_scatter

00:20:12.590 --> 00:20:14.820
is not going to
operate in place,

00:20:14.820 --> 00:20:17.000
this is just going
to be a scalar.

00:20:17.000 --> 00:20:22.940
So before the reduce_scatter,
this is what it looks like.

00:20:22.940 --> 00:20:25.670
I have my input as before.

00:20:25.670 --> 00:20:30.240
Output happens to be 0's,
but it could be any value

00:20:30.240 --> 00:20:32.110
since I didn't initialize it.

00:20:32.110 --> 00:20:36.360
And then after the
reduce_scatter,

00:20:36.360 --> 00:20:40.110
when I pass in the input and the
output, and I'm going to sum,

00:20:40.110 --> 00:20:43.260
then I get--

00:20:43.260 --> 00:20:47.730
essentially what happens is
that for the first component,

00:20:47.730 --> 00:20:54.000
I sum, and that goes on rank 0;
for the second component, I sum,

00:20:54.000 --> 00:20:56.460
and it goes on
rank 1, and so on.

00:20:56.460 --> 00:20:59.490
So as you notice, it is
producing the same operation

00:20:59.490 --> 00:21:03.930
as all_reduce except for
the output is scattered

00:21:03.930 --> 00:21:06.433
across all the different ranks.

00:21:11.040 --> 00:21:14.380
So now let's do all_gather.

00:21:14.380 --> 00:21:20.520
So I'm going to just directly
use the output reduce_scatter,

00:21:20.520 --> 00:21:25.420
which is this, as the input.

00:21:25.420 --> 00:21:31.700
And then I'm going to allocate
an empty array for the output.

00:21:31.700 --> 00:21:37.470
And then so before the
all_gather, the input is this,

00:21:37.470 --> 00:21:43.040
and the output, I guess,
are just arbitrary values.

00:21:43.040 --> 00:21:50.570
And after I do the
all_gather, what happens

00:21:50.570 --> 00:21:58.183
is I get all these tensors to
show up on all the devices.

00:22:00.800 --> 00:22:04.310
So this is just a
also an example.

00:22:04.310 --> 00:22:06.290
Hopefully now you're
very convinced

00:22:06.290 --> 00:22:08.090
that reduce_scatter
plus all_gather

00:22:08.090 --> 00:22:10.280
is just all_reduce
because I computed

00:22:10.280 --> 00:22:14.679
exactly the same quantity
as I did for all_reduce.

00:22:19.730 --> 00:22:21.140
OK.

00:22:21.140 --> 00:22:23.360
Questions?

00:22:23.360 --> 00:22:25.490
Is this clear?

00:22:25.490 --> 00:22:26.375
Yeah?

00:22:26.375 --> 00:22:29.880
in reduce_scatter, are
we keeping track of which

00:22:29.880 --> 00:22:32.130
index goes to which GPU?

00:22:32.130 --> 00:22:35.020
So the question is,
in reduced-scatter,

00:22:35.020 --> 00:22:38.680
do you keep track of which
index goes to which GPU?

00:22:38.680 --> 00:22:45.340
So by convention, the
dimensionality has to be,

00:22:45.340 --> 00:22:46.510
then, basically the--

00:22:46.510 --> 00:22:48.010
I mean it could be
a general tensor,

00:22:48.010 --> 00:22:50.440
but one of the dimensions
is the world_size,

00:22:50.440 --> 00:22:56.250
and it just infers that
basically what you want to do is

00:22:56.250 --> 00:23:00.420
the output is the--
let's say the--

00:23:00.420 --> 00:23:02.010
sorry.

00:23:02.010 --> 00:23:04.270
The input has to be
basically world_size,

00:23:04.270 --> 00:23:07.410
and then it knows
that basically,

00:23:07.410 --> 00:23:14.520
the corresponding computations
go to each of the outputs.

00:23:14.520 --> 00:23:17.310
Yeah, you have to be a bit
careful with making sure

00:23:17.310 --> 00:23:19.420
the dimensionality align.

00:23:19.420 --> 00:23:24.437
So going through this with
small examples can be helpful.

00:23:24.437 --> 00:23:25.520
Is there another question?

00:23:31.370 --> 00:23:38.310
So finally, we're now in
this process that's running,

00:23:38.310 --> 00:23:41.420
and when you're done,
you just clean up.

00:23:41.420 --> 00:23:41.920
OK.

00:23:44.750 --> 00:23:48.940
So so far, we've talked about
these collective operations,

00:23:48.940 --> 00:23:51.200
a bit about how
they're implemented

00:23:51.200 --> 00:23:53.120
in PyTorch, and its--

00:23:53.120 --> 00:23:55.280
NCCL and then PyTorch.

00:23:55.280 --> 00:24:00.920
Let's do a bit of benchmarking
in the spirit of what we did

00:24:00.920 --> 00:24:05.270
in assignment-- or
the first lecture--

00:24:05.270 --> 00:24:08.660
or rather, the second lecture.

00:24:08.660 --> 00:24:11.640
We're going to focus
on one node for now.

00:24:11.640 --> 00:24:14.940
So let's do all_reduce.

00:24:14.940 --> 00:24:20.570
So I'm going to have this
tensor of 100 million elements

00:24:20.570 --> 00:24:22.860
and a world_size of 4.

00:24:22.860 --> 00:24:28.980
So I'm going to just
allocate a tensor.

00:24:28.980 --> 00:24:32.130
And generally, as I think--

00:24:32.130 --> 00:24:36.940
as you hopefully can appreciate
now, that when you benchmark,

00:24:36.940 --> 00:24:40.020
you have to really
be careful to clean

00:24:40.020 --> 00:24:42.660
your palette in some sense.

00:24:42.660 --> 00:24:46.230
In this case, I'm going
to warm up, basically run

00:24:46.230 --> 00:24:52.270
the operation once, and then
synchronize and do barrier.

00:24:52.270 --> 00:24:53.760
Some of this is,
I think, probably

00:24:53.760 --> 00:24:56.910
a bit defensive, but
just to be safe so

00:24:56.910 --> 00:25:00.360
that all the kernels get
loaded and whatever needs

00:25:00.360 --> 00:25:02.830
to be computed gets computed.

00:25:02.830 --> 00:25:06.060
And then I'm going
to start the clock,

00:25:06.060 --> 00:25:11.000
all_reduce, and then synchronize
again, and stop the clock.

00:25:13.516 --> 00:25:19.860
So, now I can look at
how long that took.

00:25:19.860 --> 00:25:24.583
So if I scroll down here--

00:25:24.583 --> 00:25:26.000
I guess this is
not that informal.

00:25:26.000 --> 00:25:29.080
I should have printed in
microseconds, probably.

00:25:29.080 --> 00:25:32.260
It was, I guess, very quick.

00:25:32.260 --> 00:25:34.990
Some number of seconds.

00:25:34.990 --> 00:25:37.810
And now, let's measure
the bandwidth, which

00:25:37.810 --> 00:25:40.390
is the number of gigabytes
that were actually transferred

00:25:40.390 --> 00:25:43.450
in aggregate per second.

00:25:43.450 --> 00:25:47.350
So the way we do that is
we have to think about what

00:25:47.350 --> 00:25:49.070
actually gets transferred here.

00:25:49.070 --> 00:25:53.510
So, there's a tensor
with that element size,

00:25:53.510 --> 00:25:56.320
and the size of
each element is--

00:25:56.320 --> 00:26:01.342
I guess this-- I think this is
float 32, so that would be 2--

00:26:03.970 --> 00:26:05.800
sorry, for 4 bytes.

00:26:05.800 --> 00:26:15.220
And so that's the size in bytes.

00:26:15.220 --> 00:26:17.930
Now this is a little bit subtle.

00:26:17.930 --> 00:26:22.940
So how many bytes
are actually sent?

00:26:22.940 --> 00:26:24.350
Or transferred?

00:26:24.350 --> 00:26:26.840
Sent, slash,, received?

00:26:26.840 --> 00:26:33.390
So each tensor sitting
on a rank has size_bytes.

00:26:33.390 --> 00:26:37.820
And it needs to send it
to world_size minus 1

00:26:37.820 --> 00:26:40.130
in other machines--

00:26:40.130 --> 00:26:42.540
or ranks, rather.

00:26:42.540 --> 00:26:46.100
But there's a factor of 2.

00:26:46.100 --> 00:26:49.040
So why is there a factor of 2?

00:26:49.040 --> 00:26:52.130
Because you're doing an
all_reduce, remember? so you

00:26:52.130 --> 00:26:57.590
need to send all the distinct
elements into basically one

00:26:57.590 --> 00:27:00.450
place, it needs
to get summed up,

00:27:00.450 --> 00:27:05.930
and then that needs to
go back to everyone.

00:27:05.930 --> 00:27:11.330
So a rank needs to send the
input out and then receive

00:27:11.330 --> 00:27:12.060
the output.

00:27:12.060 --> 00:27:15.200
So that's why there's
a factor of 2 there.

00:27:15.200 --> 00:27:20.740
And so the total duration
is the world_size times

00:27:20.740 --> 00:27:24.410
the actual duration that passed.

00:27:24.410 --> 00:27:26.710
So I guess we're
just assuming that--

00:27:26.710 --> 00:27:29.620
if there's four
processors, that's

00:27:29.620 --> 00:27:33.500
like four times as much wall
clock time that happened.

00:27:33.500 --> 00:27:38.140
And the bandwidth is just
the bytes over the duration.

00:27:38.140 --> 00:27:42.190
OK, so what do we get here?

00:27:42.190 --> 00:27:46.730
Is about 277
gigabytes per second.

00:27:46.730 --> 00:27:55.413
So I think for at
H100 above, I think

00:27:55.413 --> 00:27:56.830
I claimed that
there was something

00:27:56.830 --> 00:28:00.110
like 900 gigabytes per second.

00:28:00.110 --> 00:28:02.500
Now of course, as we
know, your mileage

00:28:02.500 --> 00:28:04.870
varies depending on
the size of the tensors

00:28:04.870 --> 00:28:08.500
and the exact number of
devices and the weather

00:28:08.500 --> 00:28:12.860
and whatever-- no, not the
weather, but various factors.

00:28:12.860 --> 00:28:15.410
So your mileage might vary.

00:28:15.410 --> 00:28:17.350
So it's always good
to benchmark to see

00:28:17.350 --> 00:28:20.840
what is actually the
number of gigabytes

00:28:20.840 --> 00:28:22.090
per second you're getting.

00:28:24.456 --> 00:28:24.956
OK.

00:28:24.956 --> 00:28:29.745
So reduce_scatter is going
to be very, very similar,

00:28:29.745 --> 00:28:31.620
so let's just go through
this really quickly.

00:28:31.620 --> 00:28:37.020
So we created input, which is
world_size times num_elements.

00:28:37.020 --> 00:28:42.860
So each rank is going
to have the matrix.

00:28:42.860 --> 00:28:49.910
And so we're going to warm
up, and then start the clock,

00:28:49.910 --> 00:28:56.880
reduce_scatter, stop the clock,
and then see how long it took.

00:28:56.880 --> 00:28:59.150
Well, OK, that's not helpful.

00:28:59.150 --> 00:29:02.400
And then let's look
at the bandwidth.

00:29:02.400 --> 00:29:08.000
So the number of sent_bytes
is a factor of 2 here

00:29:08.000 --> 00:29:11.300
because in reduce_scatter,
remember, all you're doing

00:29:11.300 --> 00:29:17.600
is you're sending your
inputs into one place.

00:29:17.600 --> 00:29:21.430
If you just think about
reduce, all the elements just

00:29:21.430 --> 00:29:23.090
go into one place and that's it.

00:29:23.090 --> 00:29:25.960
And scatter just means
that different components

00:29:25.960 --> 00:29:28.190
of your tensor are going
to different places,

00:29:28.190 --> 00:29:33.020
but effectively
it's like a reduce.

00:29:33.020 --> 00:29:36.200
So if you do the
same calculation,

00:29:36.200 --> 00:29:37.900
you'll see that it's--

00:29:37.900 --> 00:29:41.380
I guess I get 70 in this case.

00:29:41.380 --> 00:29:45.610
So I don't exactly know why
it's exactly 70 as opposed

00:29:45.610 --> 00:29:47.470
to some other number.

00:29:47.470 --> 00:29:50.500
I guess one could speculate that
all_reduce generally there's

00:29:50.500 --> 00:29:56.020
more traffic that
happens, and all_reduce is

00:29:56.020 --> 00:29:58.220
our potentially more optimized.

00:29:58.220 --> 00:30:02.320
I think that NVIDIA hardware
has this sharp acceleration that

00:30:02.320 --> 00:30:06.520
actually does some
of these computations

00:30:06.520 --> 00:30:11.900
in the actual network, which
shaves off a factor of 2,

00:30:11.900 --> 00:30:13.510
but I don't know if
that's completely

00:30:13.510 --> 00:30:16.050
accounts for a difference here.

00:30:16.050 --> 00:30:19.440
There's a lot of stuff that
happens in NCCL that it's

00:30:19.440 --> 00:30:23.050
a little bit hard to reason
about the performance

00:30:23.050 --> 00:30:28.020
exactly, hence benchmarking.

00:30:28.020 --> 00:30:28.920
Yeah?

00:30:28.920 --> 00:30:31.380
I had a question
about the satellites--

00:30:31.380 --> 00:30:34.750
or the data bytes and how that
was calculated specifically.

00:30:34.750 --> 00:30:39.060
It looks like it calculates
just the data that's

00:30:39.060 --> 00:30:42.060
being sent to the output,
but what about the input

00:30:42.060 --> 00:30:43.980
to the reduction step?

00:30:43.980 --> 00:30:46.600
I'm wondering like how it
gets the inputs to the--

00:30:46.600 --> 00:30:50.100
So the question is it
seems like this is just

00:30:50.100 --> 00:30:54.040
the bytes for the output
and what about the input?

00:30:54.040 --> 00:30:57.450
So to be clear, I am
assuming that the inputs just

00:30:57.450 --> 00:31:01.810
are already on the device, so
I'm not counting that time.

00:31:01.810 --> 00:31:04.380
And I'm just counting
what needs to happen

00:31:04.380 --> 00:31:08.940
to do the reduce_scatter.

00:31:08.940 --> 00:31:11.850
Is this just the scatter or--

00:31:11.850 --> 00:31:14.460
This is a reduce_scatter
operation.

00:31:14.460 --> 00:31:18.990
So you need [INAUDIBLE]?

00:31:18.990 --> 00:31:23.160
So this function
does reduce_scatter.

00:31:23.160 --> 00:31:24.446
So it's one operation.

00:31:27.540 --> 00:31:31.570
I mean, like we [INAUDIBLE]
twice in the previous

00:31:31.570 --> 00:31:33.480
[INAUDIBLE] because
we were doing

00:31:33.480 --> 00:31:37.000
reduction, which accounted
for half of 2 bytes and then

00:31:37.000 --> 00:31:39.150
[INAUDIBLE].

00:31:39.150 --> 00:31:43.060
So you're saying
that for all_reduce,

00:31:43.060 --> 00:31:47.070
there's a 2x because you needed
to reduce NV and you needed

00:31:47.070 --> 00:31:50.220
to spread out again?

00:31:50.220 --> 00:31:53.200
For reduce_scatter, I
mean, it's just a name.

00:31:53.200 --> 00:31:55.620
It's called reduce_scatter,
but it's really

00:31:55.620 --> 00:31:58.526
just a row reduction.

00:32:04.290 --> 00:32:07.980
And you can also
see based on this

00:32:07.980 --> 00:32:11.800
that if you do reduce_scatter
and you do all_gather,

00:32:11.800 --> 00:32:13.862
each of those doesn't
have the factor of 2,

00:32:13.862 --> 00:32:15.820
so when you add them up,
you get a factor of 2,

00:32:15.820 --> 00:32:20.700
which is another way to see
that all_reduce is twice.

00:32:20.700 --> 00:32:26.340
And there's some references
you can go read about how

00:32:26.340 --> 00:32:30.480
to benchmark and these
collective operations.

00:32:30.480 --> 00:32:36.570
OK, so let's now talk about
the distributed training piece.

00:32:36.570 --> 00:32:39.690
So our general approach
here is going to be--

00:32:39.690 --> 00:32:41.460
I'm going to walk
through a bare bones

00:32:41.460 --> 00:32:46.600
implementation of each strategy
on deep MLPs, essentially.

00:32:46.600 --> 00:32:50.550
So recall that you generally
are in a regime where

00:32:50.550 --> 00:32:53.670
MLPs are the compute bottleneck
and transformers, not

00:32:53.670 --> 00:32:54.370
the attention.

00:32:54.370 --> 00:32:56.460
So in some ways,
even though this

00:32:56.460 --> 00:32:58.320
is a very simple
architecture, it's

00:32:58.320 --> 00:33:02.580
fairly representative
of the type of workloads

00:33:02.580 --> 00:33:05.730
that you'll see.

00:33:05.730 --> 00:33:11.370
So let's start with
data parallelism.

00:33:11.370 --> 00:33:14.520
Actually, just one note,
is that data, tensor,

00:33:14.520 --> 00:33:16.320
and pipeline parallelism are--

00:33:16.320 --> 00:33:21.216
you can just think about them
as different ways of cutting up

00:33:21.216 --> 00:33:25.200
either your model or your
data, which hopefully I'll

00:33:25.200 --> 00:33:27.510
depict visually here.

00:33:27.510 --> 00:33:32.080
OK, so in data_parallelism,
here's your model,

00:33:32.080 --> 00:33:33.580
assuming it has four layers.

00:33:33.580 --> 00:33:36.240
Each layer of the
MLP is just a matrix

00:33:36.240 --> 00:33:40.860
multiply where this is
the hidden dimension.

00:33:40.860 --> 00:33:44.340
And so the data is also
a matrix, which is--

00:33:44.340 --> 00:33:47.050
there's the batch dimension
and then the hidden dimension.

00:33:47.050 --> 00:33:50.880
And data parallel just cuts
along the batch dimension

00:33:50.880 --> 00:33:56.250
into essentially smaller pieces.

00:33:56.250 --> 00:34:02.160
So now each rank is going to get
a different slice of the data.

00:34:02.160 --> 00:34:05.610
So let's do an example here.

00:34:05.610 --> 00:34:07.450
So I'm going to generate
some sample data.

00:34:07.450 --> 00:34:10.440
So let's say I have
a batch size of 128,

00:34:10.440 --> 00:34:14.540
hidden dimension
of 1024, and then

00:34:14.540 --> 00:34:17.760
just generate some random data.

00:34:17.760 --> 00:34:20.190
So I have batch size
by number of dimension,

00:34:20.190 --> 00:34:26.870
and I'm going to run this data
parallel algorithm, or DDP.

00:34:26.870 --> 00:34:32.850
So here, I'm going to--

00:34:32.850 --> 00:34:35.400
so I got passed this data.

00:34:35.400 --> 00:34:39.650
There's a batch size and
the dimension as claimed

00:34:39.650 --> 00:34:40.830
from before.

00:34:40.830 --> 00:34:44.130
Now I divide the batch_size
by the world_size,

00:34:44.130 --> 00:34:45.719
so I get the local batch_size.

00:34:45.719 --> 00:34:52.760
That's how many-- how big the
batch size is on a given rank.

00:34:52.760 --> 00:34:55.530
And then I'm going
to, based on the rank,

00:34:55.530 --> 00:35:01.700
just figure out which starting
and ending indices of size

00:35:01.700 --> 00:35:04.700
local_batch_size I need
to access, and then

00:35:04.700 --> 00:35:07.080
get the corresponding
data from that.

00:35:07.080 --> 00:35:10.350
So basically, I'm just reaching
in and grabbing some subset

00:35:10.350 --> 00:35:13.830
of the rows based on the rank.

00:35:13.830 --> 00:35:18.270
OK, so now I'm setting
up the MLP here.

00:35:18.270 --> 00:35:24.310
And this is done very
bare bones, you could say.

00:35:24.310 --> 00:35:28.300
So here, I am creating
the MLP parameters.

00:35:28.300 --> 00:35:32.230
So each layer has
essentially a matrix,

00:35:32.230 --> 00:35:38.970
which is num_dim by a num_dim,
and remember, num_dim is 1024.

00:35:38.970 --> 00:35:41.500
And I'm going to
create the optimizer.

00:35:41.500 --> 00:35:47.580
So remember, this function
is running asynchronously

00:35:47.580 --> 00:35:50.710
on all the different--
on each rank.

00:35:50.710 --> 00:35:52.980
So each of the four ranks
is going to be running this

00:35:52.980 --> 00:35:56.490
with rank equals 0, 1, 2, 3.

00:35:56.490 --> 00:35:58.212
And now I'm going
to start training.

00:35:58.212 --> 00:35:59.670
So for a number of
steps, I'm going

00:35:59.670 --> 00:36:03.720
to do a forward pass
through the layers,

00:36:03.720 --> 00:36:07.180
matrix multiply non-linearity,
matrix multiply non-linearity.

00:36:07.180 --> 00:36:08.785
There's four layers here.

00:36:08.785 --> 00:36:10.160
We're going to
compute some loss.

00:36:10.160 --> 00:36:11.702
I don't really care
what the loss is,

00:36:11.702 --> 00:36:13.550
it's just something made up.

00:36:13.550 --> 00:36:15.320
And I'm going to do
the backward pass.

00:36:15.320 --> 00:36:21.580
So so far, this just looks
like I'm implementing SGD.

00:36:21.580 --> 00:36:23.390
And that's the point.

00:36:23.390 --> 00:36:26.200
The only difference is
now, to implement DDP,

00:36:26.200 --> 00:36:31.510
is that you just inject this
line here which synchronizes

00:36:31.510 --> 00:36:32.840
the gradients across workers.

00:36:32.840 --> 00:36:36.340
So what you do is for
each of the layers,

00:36:36.340 --> 00:36:41.350
you call an all_reduce
where you're averaging,

00:36:41.350 --> 00:36:44.020
and the thing you're
averaging is param-grad.

00:36:46.720 --> 00:36:51.700
So it's just like you've
hijacked someone's SGD code

00:36:51.700 --> 00:36:54.340
and you're saying, "Wait, I'm
actually going to just mix all

00:36:54.340 --> 00:36:57.760
the gradients after
the backward pass."

00:36:57.760 --> 00:37:00.220
And then after you
do that, you just

00:37:00.220 --> 00:37:05.140
update the parameters as usual.

00:37:05.140 --> 00:37:08.130
So from the SGD perspective, it
seems like nothing is happening,

00:37:08.130 --> 00:37:11.090
I'm just running SGD,
but someone has just

00:37:11.090 --> 00:37:14.466
mixed my gradients.

00:37:19.550 --> 00:37:27.200
So I guess just to
print out some things.

00:37:27.200 --> 00:37:31.440
So data parallel, I'm
printing out the loss.

00:37:31.440 --> 00:37:36.260
So one thing to note is that the
losses are different between all

00:37:36.260 --> 00:37:39.020
the different ranks because
they have different datas,

00:37:39.020 --> 00:37:44.005
but after the all_reduce, all
the parameters are the same.

00:37:46.580 --> 00:37:50.240
So this is [INAUDIBLE]
your textbook application

00:37:50.240 --> 00:37:53.300
of all_reduce in ML setup.

00:37:53.300 --> 00:37:55.040
Yeah?

00:37:55.040 --> 00:37:58.280
[INAUDIBLE] each rank
routes this [INAUDIBLE],

00:37:58.280 --> 00:38:02.420
how do they ensure that they
are all at the same step?

00:38:02.420 --> 00:38:03.920
Marching through the same step?

00:38:03.920 --> 00:38:05.795
Or [INAUDIBLE] it doesn't
matter [INAUDIBLE]?

00:38:05.795 --> 00:38:08.530
So the question is,
how do you ensure--

00:38:08.530 --> 00:38:12.980
if all of these processes are
just running asynchronously,

00:38:12.980 --> 00:38:15.010
how do you make sure
that each of them

00:38:15.010 --> 00:38:19.330
is actually, for example,
on the same step?

00:38:19.330 --> 00:38:23.900
This is because all_reduce
is a synchronization point.

00:38:23.900 --> 00:38:29.240
It'll stop everyone
and do the all_reduce.

00:38:29.240 --> 00:38:35.230
So you have to be careful
because if one of your ranks

00:38:35.230 --> 00:38:39.560
has a missing all_reduce,
then it will just hang.

00:38:39.560 --> 00:38:42.860
[INAUDIBLE] others will
be waiting for you--

00:38:42.860 --> 00:38:44.195
Yeah.

00:38:44.195 --> 00:38:45.975
Yeah.

00:38:45.975 --> 00:38:47.320
Yeah?

00:38:47.320 --> 00:38:50.590
Why does getting the initial
parameters depend on the rank?

00:38:50.590 --> 00:38:52.870
The question is, why does
getting initial parameters

00:38:52.870 --> 00:38:54.010
depend on the ran?

00:38:54.010 --> 00:38:55.660
Aren't they the same--

00:38:55.660 --> 00:38:56.380
they should be--

00:38:56.380 --> 00:38:57.140
They're the same.

00:38:57.140 --> 00:38:59.200
The reason is just because--

00:38:59.200 --> 00:39:00.430
I guess I don't--

00:39:00.430 --> 00:39:05.330
the code for this basically
puts it on the appropriate GPU.

00:39:05.330 --> 00:39:08.741
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:39:09.650 --> 00:39:10.170
OK.

00:39:10.170 --> 00:39:11.460
Any other questions?

00:39:18.230 --> 00:39:20.150
So DDP is something
you implement

00:39:20.150 --> 00:39:22.700
in assignment 2, which
maybe some of you

00:39:22.700 --> 00:39:26.590
have looked at or maybe not.

00:39:26.590 --> 00:39:28.590
It will be done in the
context of a transformer,

00:39:28.590 --> 00:39:32.010
but this is the most
bare bones version

00:39:32.010 --> 00:39:35.400
so you can see very
clearly what's happening.

00:39:38.360 --> 00:39:39.120
OK.

00:39:39.120 --> 00:39:40.173
So that's DDP.

00:39:43.850 --> 00:39:47.000
Losses are different
across ranks,

00:39:47.000 --> 00:39:50.340
but the gradients are
reduced to be all the same,

00:39:50.340 --> 00:39:56.580
so therefore, the parameters
of all the ranks are the same.

00:39:56.580 --> 00:40:03.100
So actually, you're doing
world_size number of SGD runs,

00:40:03.100 --> 00:40:04.720
but because they're
synchronized,

00:40:04.720 --> 00:40:06.250
they're doing the same thing.

00:40:06.250 --> 00:40:10.830
So you can think about this
as an instantiation of analog

00:40:10.830 --> 00:40:13.920
of activation checkpointing
where sometimes you

00:40:13.920 --> 00:40:16.960
just do extra compute because
you don't want to store things.

00:40:16.960 --> 00:40:19.262
In this case, we could
have, for example,

00:40:19.262 --> 00:40:20.970
ship the optimizer
state around, but that

00:40:20.970 --> 00:40:25.560
would be a bad idea because it's
much faster just to run the--

00:40:25.560 --> 00:40:30.570
update the optimizer state than
to actually move the optimizer

00:40:30.570 --> 00:40:31.385
parameters around.

00:40:34.050 --> 00:40:35.080
OK.

00:40:35.080 --> 00:40:38.370
So last year, I
did try to do FSDP,

00:40:38.370 --> 00:40:44.700
but that was a hairball, so
I'm going to skip that and do

00:40:44.700 --> 00:40:46.800
a tensor parallel.

00:40:46.800 --> 00:40:49.830
So here, the picture is--

00:40:49.830 --> 00:40:52.150
we leave the data the same.

00:40:52.150 --> 00:40:53.910
And now what we're
going to do is

00:40:53.910 --> 00:41:00.180
we're going to cut the model
along the hidden dimension.

00:41:02.900 --> 00:41:06.120
So each rank is going
to get every layer,

00:41:06.120 --> 00:41:08.900
but it's going to get
only part of each layer.

00:41:08.900 --> 00:41:11.390
And what we're going
to end up doing

00:41:11.390 --> 00:41:16.068
is transfer all the data
and the activations around.

00:41:16.068 --> 00:41:16.568
OK.

00:41:16.568 --> 00:41:21.320
So we're generating
the same sample data,

00:41:21.320 --> 00:41:25.640
and let's look at
tensor parallel.

00:41:25.640 --> 00:41:32.250
OK, so I have the batch_size
and num_dim as before.

00:41:32.250 --> 00:41:35.540
And now, I'm going to--

00:41:35.540 --> 00:41:39.210
before I was cutting batch_size,
but now I'm cutting num_dim.

00:41:39.210 --> 00:41:47.610
So I have local num_dim equals
1024 divided by world_size.

00:41:47.610 --> 00:41:49.370
And that's 256.

00:41:49.370 --> 00:41:54.170
So each model essentially--

00:41:54.170 --> 00:41:54.720
sorry.

00:41:54.720 --> 00:41:57.630
Each rank gets a
part of the model,

00:41:57.630 --> 00:42:02.340
which is 1 over the world_size
fraction of the parameters.

00:42:02.340 --> 00:42:05.520
And remember, why we're
doing parallelism at all

00:42:05.520 --> 00:42:09.640
is because the model won't be
able to fit into a single GPU,

00:42:09.640 --> 00:42:14.340
so we're going to shard
it across multiple GPUs.

00:42:14.340 --> 00:42:23.190
So the parameter matrices are
now num_dim by local_num_dim.

00:42:23.190 --> 00:42:26.260
And now, each rank is going to--

00:42:26.260 --> 00:42:29.310
I'm only going to implement
the forward pass here,

00:42:29.310 --> 00:42:32.130
not the whole training loop.

00:42:32.130 --> 00:42:36.420
So I'm going to start going
through all the layers.

00:42:36.420 --> 00:42:41.800
So I'm going to compute
the activations first.

00:42:41.800 --> 00:42:44.580
So this looks pretty
normal, except for,

00:42:44.580 --> 00:42:46.740
remember, the
activations are actually

00:42:46.740 --> 00:42:50.070
batch size by local_num_dim
rather than num_dim

00:42:50.070 --> 00:42:53.160
because I only each
rank only has a fraction

00:42:53.160 --> 00:42:55.950
of the activations now.

00:42:55.950 --> 00:42:59.590
But now, once I get
the activations,

00:42:59.590 --> 00:43:02.800
I need to communicate.

00:43:02.800 --> 00:43:08.470
And here, what I
have to do is I'm

00:43:08.470 --> 00:43:11.240
going to allocate memory
for all the activations.

00:43:11.240 --> 00:43:15.613
So at this point,
every one has an

00:43:15.613 --> 00:43:21.610
x, but that x represents
a different part

00:43:21.610 --> 00:43:23.570
of the activations.

00:43:23.570 --> 00:43:26.590
So now I'm going
to just allocate

00:43:26.590 --> 00:43:31.190
batch_size and local_num_dim,
but world_size number.

00:43:31.190 --> 00:43:36.190
So basically, each rank is
going to basically have enough--

00:43:36.190 --> 00:43:37.750
I'm going to just get--

00:43:40.340 --> 00:43:43.240
basically have world_size
number of batch_size

00:43:43.240 --> 00:43:47.110
by local_num_dim matrices.

00:43:47.110 --> 00:43:52.960
And then I'm going
to do an all_gather.

00:43:52.960 --> 00:43:59.250
So I'm going to send
all the activations.

00:43:59.250 --> 00:44:02.800
And this-- I mean,
it's fairly simple.

00:44:02.800 --> 00:44:06.880
So x, remember, is batch_size
times local_num_dim,

00:44:06.880 --> 00:44:09.370
but x is different
for every rank.

00:44:09.370 --> 00:44:11.730
So when I do the
all_gather, I'm going

00:44:11.730 --> 00:44:15.540
to put it in
activations, which has

00:44:15.540 --> 00:44:22.240
essentially a world_size
number of the same shape as x.

00:44:22.240 --> 00:44:28.000
So now every rank has
the same activations--

00:44:28.000 --> 00:44:30.810
now it has the activations
of all the models--

00:44:30.810 --> 00:44:33.520
of the whole model.

00:44:33.520 --> 00:44:39.480
And then just to concatenate
them together to get x.

00:44:39.480 --> 00:44:48.790
OK, so now x is now, again,
batch_size by num_dim

00:44:48.790 --> 00:44:50.090
And I repeat.

00:44:53.250 --> 00:44:56.710
So as you can see, this
is-- there's quite a bit

00:44:56.710 --> 00:44:59.950
of communication that
happens, which is why--

00:44:59.950 --> 00:45:02.200
remember, Tatsu said
that for tensor parallel,

00:45:02.200 --> 00:45:04.970
you need pretty
high interconnects,

00:45:04.970 --> 00:45:08.385
otherwise you'd be passing a
lot of these activations around.

00:45:11.080 --> 00:45:14.090
And then you do it for the
next layer, and the next layer,

00:45:14.090 --> 00:45:15.720
and you get the idea.

00:45:18.430 --> 00:45:21.590
And just to print
out some output.

00:45:21.590 --> 00:45:26.950
So tensor parallel,
let's see here.

00:45:26.950 --> 00:45:29.350
Forward pass
produces activations

00:45:29.350 --> 00:45:34.060
of basically the full
size, and everyone

00:45:34.060 --> 00:45:36.960
has the same
activations at the end.

00:45:42.910 --> 00:45:45.940
So backward pass
I'm going to skip

00:45:45.940 --> 00:45:48.870
because that's annoying to do.

00:45:51.790 --> 00:45:53.750
All right.

00:45:53.750 --> 00:45:54.895
Any questions about that?

00:46:00.260 --> 00:46:01.100
Yeah?

00:46:01.100 --> 00:46:04.190
I was just wondering why
it's hard to do [INAUDIBLE]?

00:46:04.190 --> 00:46:05.940
So why is it hard to
do the backward pass?

00:46:05.940 --> 00:46:08.460
I don't think it's
necessarily hard,

00:46:08.460 --> 00:46:15.036
but I guess in the
constrained time and space--

00:46:15.036 --> 00:46:18.520
it's not hard, it's just
requires a bit more work.

00:46:23.326 --> 00:46:23.826
OK.

00:46:27.224 --> 00:46:29.760
So now let's go to
pipeline parallelism.

00:46:29.760 --> 00:46:35.280
So in this case, we're
cutting the model by layers.

00:46:35.280 --> 00:46:43.160
So all the ranks get all
the data and all the ranks--

00:46:43.160 --> 00:46:48.800
each rank gets all of one layer,
but they get different layers

00:46:48.800 --> 00:46:57.700
OK, so sample the data, and run
this function for all the ranks.

00:46:57.700 --> 00:46:58.906
OK.

00:46:58.906 --> 00:47:05.650
So here, I'm going to figure
out how many layers go

00:47:05.650 --> 00:47:12.020
in each rank, which is 2 here.

00:47:12.020 --> 00:47:14.080
So I have a four-layer network.

00:47:14.080 --> 00:47:22.660
I have two ranks, so each
rank gets two of the layers--

00:47:22.660 --> 00:47:26.410
just like this
picture, actually.

00:47:26.410 --> 00:47:32.080
And here, I'm going to just
allocate the parameters just

00:47:32.080 --> 00:47:34.750
for the layers that I need.

00:47:38.800 --> 00:47:43.210
So I'm going to do
the forward pass.

00:47:43.210 --> 00:47:48.200
Remember, there was a further
optimization that you do,

00:47:48.200 --> 00:47:54.420
which is if you
just do it naively,

00:47:54.420 --> 00:47:58.910
you get these pipeline bubbles
that Tatsu talked about before.

00:47:58.910 --> 00:48:02.390
One way to mitigate
that is to break up

00:48:02.390 --> 00:48:04.170
the batch into micro-batches.

00:48:04.170 --> 00:48:09.590
So here, I'm going
to divide this batch

00:48:09.590 --> 00:48:14.490
into batches of size 32.

00:48:14.490 --> 00:48:17.360
So 4 batches of size 32.

00:48:17.360 --> 00:48:21.950
And then-- now the
idea is that every rank

00:48:21.950 --> 00:48:25.790
is going to essentially
wait for the previous rank

00:48:25.790 --> 00:48:28.620
to pass it to the activations.

00:48:28.620 --> 00:48:30.350
It's going to apply
those layers and then

00:48:30.350 --> 00:48:33.150
it's going to forward
it to the next rank.

00:48:33.150 --> 00:48:36.750
So starting at the base
case, we have rank equals 0.

00:48:36.750 --> 00:48:40.040
That's just the data.

00:48:40.040 --> 00:48:46.070
So I'm just chunking the data
into a bunch of micro-batches.

00:48:46.070 --> 00:48:51.800
And going through each
of the micro-batches,

00:48:51.800 --> 00:48:53.970
first I receive the tensor.

00:48:53.970 --> 00:48:57.200
So I'm using these
point-to-point primitives

00:48:57.200 --> 00:49:02.330
now instead of the
collective primitives.

00:49:02.330 --> 00:49:09.245
And I essentially-- they
basically received the tensor x.

00:49:09.245 --> 00:49:11.120
And then I'm going to
compute the layers that

00:49:11.120 --> 00:49:12.570
are assigned to this rank.

00:49:12.570 --> 00:49:15.500
So in this case, there's
only two of them.

00:49:15.500 --> 00:49:19.705
And then I'm going to
send it to the next rank.

00:49:22.280 --> 00:49:27.500
And then, again, send is a
point-to-point operation.

00:49:27.500 --> 00:49:33.390
And then the next batch, I'm
going to do the same thing,

00:49:33.390 --> 00:49:36.590
so I'm going to skip that.

00:49:36.590 --> 00:49:38.430
OK, so that's basically it.

00:49:38.430 --> 00:49:42.530
So pipeline parallel, at least
the very naive version of it,

00:49:42.530 --> 00:49:44.910
is relatively
conceptually simple.

00:49:44.910 --> 00:49:46.880
But it's not to
mention last time,

00:49:46.880 --> 00:49:49.100
there's many things
that are missing

00:49:49.100 --> 00:49:52.220
from this basic implementation.

00:49:52.220 --> 00:49:54.650
Overlapping the
communication and computation

00:49:54.650 --> 00:49:58.820
is something we're
not doing at all here.

00:49:58.820 --> 00:50:01.590
For example, receive and
send are synchronous,

00:50:01.590 --> 00:50:03.540
but you should really
make them async.

00:50:03.540 --> 00:50:07.760
And also, the order in
which you do the forward--

00:50:07.760 --> 00:50:10.410
actually, this is just a
forward, even, not the backward,

00:50:10.410 --> 00:50:12.560
but once you have the
backward, then you

00:50:12.560 --> 00:50:15.350
have to figure out how
to interleave the forward

00:50:15.350 --> 00:50:16.675
and backward steps.

00:50:21.770 --> 00:50:22.350
Yeah?

00:50:22.350 --> 00:50:24.830
[INAUDIBLE]-- I guess, maybe
what you just mentioned about

00:50:24.830 --> 00:50:27.380
the async being
shown here, is some--

00:50:27.380 --> 00:50:30.440
I guess in actuality,
like, the GPU would

00:50:30.440 --> 00:50:34.040
be listening whether another
one passes something to it,

00:50:34.040 --> 00:50:41.420
and it's [INAUDIBLE]
it only starts

00:50:41.420 --> 00:50:44.030
processing once the
[INAUDIBLE] layer

00:50:44.030 --> 00:50:48.100
before it passes [INAUDIBLE]

00:50:48.100 --> 00:50:51.390
So the question is, is this
like event-driven programming

00:50:51.390 --> 00:50:56.400
where you're just waiting
for things to happen?

00:50:56.400 --> 00:50:59.230
And I think in
event-driven programming,

00:50:59.230 --> 00:51:01.030
you basically write
these handlers,

00:51:01.030 --> 00:51:04.450
and then whenever stuff happens,
maybe you get a mouse click,

00:51:04.450 --> 00:51:08.310
maybe you get a file ready
event, then a piece of code

00:51:08.310 --> 00:51:09.040
runs.

00:51:09.040 --> 00:51:11.520
That's quite different,
I think, from this style

00:51:11.520 --> 00:51:17.190
of coding where everything
has to work in lockstep.

00:51:17.190 --> 00:51:24.600
It is true that you're
waiting for the previous rank

00:51:24.600 --> 00:51:27.700
to send you the information, but
at least in this implementation,

00:51:27.700 --> 00:51:30.945
there's no flexibility of
where it's getting from.

00:51:30.945 --> 00:51:32.820
It's not like it's
waiting for arbitrary data

00:51:32.820 --> 00:51:35.160
to come from anywhere.

00:51:35.160 --> 00:51:40.060
I think there are ways to
do asynchronous training,

00:51:40.060 --> 00:51:46.110
which was, I think, quite
popular, more than 10 years ago,

00:51:46.110 --> 00:51:51.500
where it's more event-driven
where you have a server that

00:51:51.500 --> 00:51:53.790
sends data, and whenever
the gradients are ready,

00:51:53.790 --> 00:51:57.450
it just uploads and then the
gradients get accumulated,

00:51:57.450 --> 00:52:00.440
and if workers
die, then that's--

00:52:00.440 --> 00:52:03.930
then that's handled
more robustly.

00:52:03.930 --> 00:52:10.160
But in modern training,
despite scaling up quite a bit,

00:52:10.160 --> 00:52:13.603
everything seems to be in
a synchronous paradigm.

00:52:17.120 --> 00:52:22.910
Yeah, so it is true that
when I say the workers are--

00:52:22.910 --> 00:52:25.230
and the ranks are
operating asynchronously,

00:52:25.230 --> 00:52:27.870
that's just because it's
different processes.

00:52:27.870 --> 00:52:31.100
But you're still putting
quite rigid synchronization

00:52:31.100 --> 00:52:35.420
on how everything is
working in lockstep.

00:52:35.420 --> 00:52:36.620
Yeah?

00:52:36.620 --> 00:52:40.280
I'm just curious, how would
you change this program

00:52:40.280 --> 00:52:42.570
to handle [INAUDIBLE]
layers [INAUDIBLE]

00:52:42.570 --> 00:52:45.965
computation with
[INAUDIBLE] data?

00:52:45.965 --> 00:52:47.340
So the question
is, how would you

00:52:47.340 --> 00:52:50.260
change this to overlap
communication and computation?

00:52:50.260 --> 00:52:53.500
So, for example,
when you send this,

00:52:53.500 --> 00:52:57.580
there's no reason to just
wait for the data to be sent.

00:52:57.580 --> 00:53:01.020
You just basically
fire off the send.

00:53:01.020 --> 00:53:03.390
Remember that the
send actually happens

00:53:03.390 --> 00:53:07.950
on the GPU via some kernel
launch, so that's independent.

00:53:07.950 --> 00:53:12.910
And it can just go and process
another micro-batch right away.

00:53:12.910 --> 00:53:15.150
So the way, I
think, you would do

00:53:15.150 --> 00:53:18.000
this is there's another
function called i.send

00:53:18.000 --> 00:53:22.860
which is asynchronous.

00:53:22.860 --> 00:53:26.220
Actually, this should
be synchronous.

00:53:26.220 --> 00:53:29.020
Asynchronous, which
returns a handle.

00:53:29.020 --> 00:53:32.760
And so you basically do all
the send, and then at the end,

00:53:32.760 --> 00:53:38.490
you basically wait for
all the sends to complete.

00:53:38.490 --> 00:53:41.950
And then for overlapping the--

00:53:41.950 --> 00:53:44.510
when you actually have
the backward step,

00:53:44.510 --> 00:53:48.780
then you basically have
to schedule that in here.

00:53:51.550 --> 00:53:53.260
Yeah?

00:53:53.260 --> 00:53:55.510
[INAUDIBLE]

00:54:02.830 --> 00:54:06.700
So the question is, if you have
multiple sends and multiple

00:54:06.700 --> 00:54:10.160
receives, how do you
know which is which?

00:54:10.160 --> 00:54:13.610
So here, the tensor
name doesn't matter.

00:54:13.610 --> 00:54:17.960
It's just whatever
variable is there.

00:54:17.960 --> 00:54:21.680
And what you're
specifying is the source.

00:54:21.680 --> 00:54:26.080
So if I'm at a node and I'm
receiving, then whatever

00:54:26.080 --> 00:54:29.840
the next message
coming from that rank,

00:54:29.840 --> 00:54:37.030
I'm just going to put in this
x and continue executing.

00:54:37.030 --> 00:54:40.950
What if I want to do two
sends from the same rank?

00:54:40.950 --> 00:54:47.730
If you want to do two
sends from the same rank.

00:54:47.730 --> 00:54:49.690
To the same destination?

00:54:49.690 --> 00:54:52.070
[INAUDIBLE]

00:54:58.815 --> 00:55:00.190
So I'm not quite
sure about this,

00:55:00.190 --> 00:55:04.120
but I think if you have two
sends, it's put in a stream.

00:55:04.120 --> 00:55:07.060
So the order of the
sends still is preserved.

00:55:07.060 --> 00:55:10.900
It's just that other stuff
can happen at the same time.

00:55:10.900 --> 00:55:14.130
You can send to--

00:55:14.130 --> 00:55:17.670
I think if you
have a pair and do

00:55:17.670 --> 00:55:19.450
two sends, then that
order is preserved,

00:55:19.450 --> 00:55:23.790
but the order in
which you send--

00:55:23.790 --> 00:55:26.590
some other rank is
sending to another rank,

00:55:26.590 --> 00:55:27.785
it can happen at any time.

00:55:32.520 --> 00:55:33.990
Yeah?

00:55:33.990 --> 00:55:36.090
What would happen if you
just did [INAUDIBLE],

00:55:36.090 --> 00:55:39.940
but then no one's receiving
it [INAUDIBLE] gets up there--

00:55:39.940 --> 00:55:43.430
So what happens if you send
and no one receives it?

00:55:43.430 --> 00:55:46.720
I think it would just
stop, we just wait.

00:55:46.720 --> 00:55:50.260
Because there's no-- yeah.

00:55:50.260 --> 00:55:52.030
I mean, because--

00:55:52.030 --> 00:55:56.410
I mean, the process could just
be running and you don't know

00:55:56.410 --> 00:55:58.270
whether it will-- it's just--

00:55:58.270 --> 00:55:59.900
I mean, it's just
code executing.

00:55:59.900 --> 00:56:02.260
So you don't know if it's
never going to get there

00:56:02.260 --> 00:56:05.560
or if it's just going
to be a matter of time.

00:56:05.560 --> 00:56:06.704
Yeah?

00:56:06.704 --> 00:56:09.610
[INAUDIBLE]

00:56:09.610 --> 00:56:12.740
So the question is, what
happens to the last rank?

00:56:12.740 --> 00:56:17.390
So at the end, the last
rank has all the activation.

00:56:17.390 --> 00:56:21.260
So that has basically the
results of a full forward pass.

00:56:21.260 --> 00:56:23.950
And then if you implement
the backward pass,

00:56:23.950 --> 00:56:27.910
then you would be actually
now computing the gradient

00:56:27.910 --> 00:56:30.970
with respect to the loss, and
then you would go back down

00:56:30.970 --> 00:56:35.505
and send from rank to
rank minus 1 and so on.

00:56:42.380 --> 00:56:43.340
OK.

00:56:43.340 --> 00:56:44.787
I guess maybe--

00:56:44.787 --> 00:56:46.620
I was afraid I was going
to run out of time,

00:56:46.620 --> 00:56:49.110
but it looks like I
actually have time.

00:56:49.110 --> 00:56:53.510
Maybe next year I should
do the backward pass.

00:56:53.510 --> 00:56:54.150
OK.

00:56:54.150 --> 00:56:57.450
So actually, I'm going to
finish quite early today,

00:56:57.450 --> 00:57:00.950
so if you have any other
questions, you should ask.

00:57:00.950 --> 00:57:05.630
So so far, we've gone through
three simple examples of data,

00:57:05.630 --> 00:57:08.240
tensor, pipeline parallel.

00:57:08.240 --> 00:57:12.290
Of course, this is
for simple MLPs.

00:57:12.290 --> 00:57:17.750
You would actually want to do
this with your own fancier model

00:57:17.750 --> 00:57:19.820
like a transformer.

00:57:19.820 --> 00:57:23.290
I did argue that, at least at--

00:57:23.290 --> 00:57:28.520
the core ideas you can
understand through the MLP.

00:57:28.520 --> 00:57:32.640
I think the-- but of course,
when you want to train,

00:57:32.640 --> 00:57:35.560
you want to train
transformer, not a deep MLP.

00:57:35.560 --> 00:57:40.060
So you still have to
implement the full complexity.

00:57:40.060 --> 00:57:44.500
What's also missing is the
communication and computation

00:57:44.500 --> 00:57:49.210
overlap, which is not really
handled very carefully here.

00:57:49.210 --> 00:57:53.720
And there is generally a more
complex code with bookkeeping.

00:57:53.720 --> 00:57:58.870
I encourage you to check out
Megatron-LM or PyTorch's FSDP.

00:57:58.870 --> 00:58:02.960
It gets fairly hairy.

00:58:02.960 --> 00:58:05.620
And one of the
things that, I think,

00:58:05.620 --> 00:58:08.980
makes some of the bookkeeping,
at least for, let's say, FSDP--

00:58:08.980 --> 00:58:12.380
and you'll be exposed
to this A2 a bit,

00:58:12.380 --> 00:58:16.270
is that if you want something
that handles arbitrary

00:58:16.270 --> 00:58:20.470
architectures, then you have
to figure out the parameters

00:58:20.470 --> 00:58:22.600
and do a bunch of
bookkeeping to--

00:58:22.600 --> 00:58:25.640
and figure out whether
the layers are and so on.

00:58:25.640 --> 00:58:28.300
Whereas in the MLP
case, it's just

00:58:28.300 --> 00:58:30.190
I've made the decision
that I'm going

00:58:30.190 --> 00:58:35.370
to split the model in this
particularly simple way.

00:58:35.370 --> 00:58:38.590
One other thing I'll
just mention as an aside

00:58:38.590 --> 00:58:42.280
is that all of what we're doing
in this course is PyTorch,

00:58:42.280 --> 00:58:46.680
but it is useful to be aware
of this whole other ecosystem

00:58:46.680 --> 00:58:54.270
around Jax and TPUs, which
is actually nice in some way.

00:58:54.270 --> 00:59:00.580
And the idea here is Jax allows
you to just define the model.

00:59:00.580 --> 00:59:02.800
It defines the
sharding strategy,

00:59:02.800 --> 00:59:06.580
and then the Jax compiler
handles the rest.

00:59:06.580 --> 00:59:10.110
So there's this toolkit
that we developed

00:59:10.110 --> 00:59:13.110
called Levanter based on Jax.

00:59:13.110 --> 00:59:17.320
And I'll just show you a
snippet of what happened.

00:59:17.320 --> 00:59:19.870
So this is FSDP and
10 lines of code.

00:59:19.870 --> 00:59:29.580
And basically, have a model,
and then you just say shard

00:59:29.580 --> 00:59:30.840
with this particular--

00:59:30.840 --> 00:59:33.970
I mean, I don't expect
you to read this exactly,

00:59:33.970 --> 00:59:36.840
but basically, you
define which dimension

00:59:36.840 --> 00:59:41.500
you're going to shard
by, and then that's it.

00:59:41.500 --> 00:59:45.060
And similarly for
tensor parallel,

00:59:45.060 --> 00:59:51.900
you're just saying I'm going
to shard the model along the--

00:59:51.900 --> 00:59:57.910
you can shard on the head
dimension for attention,

00:59:57.910 --> 01:00:02.610
and also, you can shard
based on the model dimension.

01:00:02.610 --> 01:00:09.990
So in some sense, this gives
you a conceptual simplicity

01:00:09.990 --> 01:00:14.190
of what you're trying
to do is you have this--

01:00:14.190 --> 01:00:21.000
basically a computation graph,
but it has these dimensions,

01:00:21.000 --> 01:00:23.200
the model dimensions,
the embedding dimension,

01:00:23.200 --> 01:00:25.690
the attention
sequence dimension.

01:00:25.690 --> 01:00:30.890
And Jax allows you to
basically just specify

01:00:30.890 --> 01:00:33.020
which dimensions
you want to cut by

01:00:33.020 --> 01:00:37.230
and also define a mapping from
that onto the actual TPUs.

01:00:37.230 --> 01:00:39.920
And then, the Jax
compiler magically

01:00:39.920 --> 01:00:42.350
just figures out
how to compile that

01:00:42.350 --> 01:00:47.040
down into the primitives
that shuffle things around.

01:00:47.040 --> 01:00:48.890
So this is much
more higher level

01:00:48.890 --> 01:00:55.550
than operating with a
collective communication.

01:00:55.550 --> 01:01:01.130
But we're sticking
with PyTorch because it

01:01:01.130 --> 01:01:04.760
allows you to see a underneath
the hood what's actually

01:01:04.760 --> 01:01:05.610
happening.

01:01:05.610 --> 01:01:08.720
But if you're actually doing
this in the real world,

01:01:08.720 --> 01:01:10.130
obviously you don't need to--

01:01:10.130 --> 01:01:11.630
and you probably
shouldn't implement

01:01:11.630 --> 01:01:13.515
all of this from scratch.

01:01:13.515 --> 01:01:14.015
OK.

01:01:14.015 --> 01:01:18.350
So that's the end of
the Jax digression.

01:01:18.350 --> 01:01:26.210
So to just summarize, we've seen
many ways to parallelize so far.

01:01:26.210 --> 01:01:28.670
And each of these ways
of parallelizing you

01:01:28.670 --> 01:01:31.080
can think about
just like splitting

01:01:31.080 --> 01:01:33.780
either the model
or the data along

01:01:33.780 --> 01:01:36.730
some dimension-- either the
data, the batch dimension,

01:01:36.730 --> 01:01:39.900
or the width dimension, or the
depth dimension, or the context

01:01:39.900 --> 01:01:41.970
length dimension.

01:01:41.970 --> 01:01:48.790
We also see this recurring
theme of computation.

01:01:48.790 --> 01:01:55.900
You can recompute
something from scratch,

01:01:55.900 --> 01:02:00.300
or you can store it in memory
and suffer the data transfer

01:02:00.300 --> 01:02:01.170
cost.

01:02:01.170 --> 01:02:04.960
Or, in now in a multi-GPU,
multi-node setting,

01:02:04.960 --> 01:02:07.440
you can actually store
on another GPU's memory

01:02:07.440 --> 01:02:12.510
and then communicate,
which is even slower.

01:02:12.510 --> 01:02:17.440
So there's these
trade-offs here.

01:02:17.440 --> 01:02:25.960
And often, re-computation
actually can be better,

01:02:25.960 --> 01:02:30.140
but obviously you can't
compute the whole thing.

01:02:30.140 --> 01:02:31.990
And often, you're
either communication- or

01:02:31.990 --> 01:02:34.450
memory-limited.

01:02:34.450 --> 01:02:37.120
The final word is that--

01:02:37.120 --> 01:02:40.460
it is the case that
hardware is getting better.

01:02:40.460 --> 01:02:43.180
So you might think that,
well, maybe none of this

01:02:43.180 --> 01:02:45.400
is really necessary
because in five years,

01:02:45.400 --> 01:02:49.040
everything will
fit in L1 or HBM.

01:02:49.040 --> 01:02:54.820
So this is not going to be the
case because those might grow

01:02:54.820 --> 01:02:58.630
quite a bit, although there
are still physical limits,

01:02:58.630 --> 01:03:01.930
will always be ending up
with bigger models that

01:03:01.930 --> 01:03:05.600
are at the limit of what
the hardware can do.

01:03:05.600 --> 01:03:11.350
So this hierarchical structure,
ever since computer systems

01:03:11.350 --> 01:03:14.990
was a thing, has
always been with us,

01:03:14.990 --> 01:03:17.980
and it will always be there.

01:03:17.980 --> 01:03:18.700
OK.

01:03:18.700 --> 01:03:21.500
That's all I have for you today.

01:03:21.500 --> 01:03:26.430
So I can take any questions.

01:03:26.430 --> 01:03:27.390
Yeah?

01:03:27.390 --> 01:03:33.270
So [INAUDIBLE] the same set of
parameters, the forward pass

01:03:33.270 --> 01:03:36.660
may be different because
you're not [INAUDIBLE]

01:03:36.660 --> 01:03:39.690
might be a function
of the input data set?

01:03:39.690 --> 01:03:46.620
So for example, when you have
[INAUDIBLE], so then [INAUDIBLE]

01:03:46.620 --> 01:03:50.550
training process [INAUDIBLE]

01:03:50.550 --> 01:03:53.910
So the question is
in data parallel,

01:03:53.910 --> 01:03:57.180
you're saying that even
though the parameters are all

01:03:57.180 --> 01:03:59.640
synchronized, there could
be other things that

01:03:59.640 --> 01:04:03.240
depend on the data,
like in batch norm?

01:04:03.240 --> 01:04:06.360
So I don't actually
know how you--

01:04:06.360 --> 01:04:09.090
batch norm is always
kind of annoying.

01:04:09.090 --> 01:04:12.120
So I don't know exactly
how you would do that

01:04:12.120 --> 01:04:13.770
off the top of my head.

01:04:13.770 --> 01:04:17.620
And I guess, at least
in the LM world,

01:04:17.620 --> 01:04:23.340
that doesn't really show up
because layer norm is used.

01:04:23.340 --> 01:04:26.530
And as long as you
initialize all them

01:04:26.530 --> 01:04:30.320
parameters and using the same
random seed, you'll be fine.

01:04:30.320 --> 01:04:33.580
I mean, there could be
non-determinism issues

01:04:33.580 --> 01:04:39.340
on the GPU, but hopefully
those are minor.

01:04:39.340 --> 01:04:40.720
Yeah?

01:04:40.720 --> 01:04:42.850
[INAUDIBLE]

01:04:50.260 --> 01:04:56.670
So the question is, does PyTorch
have some niceties as well,

01:04:56.670 --> 01:04:59.620
kind of like what Jax offers?

01:04:59.620 --> 01:05:00.310
Is that--

01:05:00.310 --> 01:05:01.000
Yeah.

01:05:01.000 --> 01:05:05.480
So, I mean, PyTorch does
have the FSDP Library,

01:05:05.480 --> 01:05:10.390
which you should absolutely use
if you're not taking this class.

01:05:10.390 --> 01:05:12.020
Which basically is a wrapper.

01:05:12.020 --> 01:05:16.360
You define any model and
it just does FSDP on it.

01:05:16.360 --> 01:05:22.360
I think that-- now, if you're
asking how well it can more

01:05:22.360 --> 01:05:23.330
custom--

01:05:23.330 --> 01:05:25.650
allow you to more
do custom sharding,

01:05:25.650 --> 01:05:29.130
I think there are some
things that are coming,

01:05:29.130 --> 01:05:33.410
but it's not as, I
think, as developed.

01:05:33.410 --> 01:05:38.090
I mean, I think there's this, I
think, spectrum between the Jax

01:05:38.090 --> 01:05:40.350
world where you
declaratively define things,

01:05:40.350 --> 01:05:43.080
and I think the
Google infrastructure,

01:05:43.080 --> 01:05:45.840
if you stay within
the Jax TPU system,

01:05:45.840 --> 01:05:47.760
it's pretty well-developed.

01:05:47.760 --> 01:05:51.030
But then if you
look at of DeepSeek,

01:05:51.030 --> 01:05:57.260
which is the opposite end where
you have these GPUs, which

01:05:57.260 --> 01:06:00.240
actually really
bad interconnect,

01:06:00.240 --> 01:06:04.040
which means that they
have to go in and--

01:06:04.040 --> 01:06:05.930
they actually go
to the NCCL level

01:06:05.930 --> 01:06:07.800
and actually do a
bunch of things,

01:06:07.800 --> 01:06:11.640
which I don't quite understand,
to eke out the performance.

01:06:11.640 --> 01:06:14.930
Whereas if you're running a
Jax, you just, from on high,

01:06:14.930 --> 01:06:18.240
declare your model and
then stuff happens.

01:06:18.240 --> 01:06:24.160
So the ways that you leverage
hardware I think really

01:06:24.160 --> 01:06:29.110
depends on what ecosystem
you're operating in.

01:06:29.110 --> 01:06:30.490
Yeah?

01:06:30.490 --> 01:06:32.620
[INAUDIBLE]

01:06:42.960 --> 01:06:43.460
Yeah.

01:06:43.460 --> 01:06:47.090
So the question is activation
checkpointing what--

01:06:47.090 --> 01:06:51.190
there is an API that
basically allows you to--

01:06:51.190 --> 01:06:52.960
I mean, I guess in
PyTorch and Jax,

01:06:52.960 --> 01:06:57.620
too, specify which parts
you want to recompute,

01:06:57.620 --> 01:07:00.250
because clearly you
don't want to recompute

01:07:00.250 --> 01:07:03.040
everything or nothing.

01:07:03.040 --> 01:07:08.240
Probably every few layers,
probably right after big MAMLs

01:07:08.240 --> 01:07:09.100
where--

01:07:09.100 --> 01:07:11.380
For example, if you
have, let's say,

01:07:11.380 --> 01:07:15.590
a MAML and then
pointwise nonlinearity,

01:07:15.590 --> 01:07:19.120
I don't think you need
to store two copies of--

01:07:19.120 --> 01:07:21.590
but basically, if you
have two things where

01:07:21.590 --> 01:07:26.720
it's trivial to get to,
then you might as well just

01:07:26.720 --> 01:07:28.285
store one version.

01:07:31.220 --> 01:07:32.840
Yeah, over there.

01:07:32.840 --> 01:07:34.445
[INAUDIBLE]

01:07:39.080 --> 01:07:41.270
So the question
is, are GPUs going

01:07:41.270 --> 01:07:45.230
to ever be replaced by a
transformer-specific hardware?

01:07:45.230 --> 01:07:50.120
So you're seeing this in the
inference space quite a bit

01:07:50.120 --> 01:07:52.380
already with--

01:07:52.380 --> 01:07:56.330
like, Grok and Cerebras have
specialized hardware that

01:07:56.330 --> 01:07:59.030
can do inference-- and
also, I guess, training,

01:07:59.030 --> 01:08:01.400
Cerebras has training.

01:08:01.400 --> 01:08:06.800
So basically, those
hardwares essentially

01:08:06.800 --> 01:08:11.300
give you just a lot
more on-chip memory.

01:08:11.300 --> 01:08:14.250
I mean, that's basically
the name of the game.

01:08:14.250 --> 01:08:16.609
I think Cerebras has a huge--

01:08:16.609 --> 01:08:20.050
essentially
effectively an L1 cache

01:08:20.050 --> 01:08:22.340
so you don't have
to move things off.

01:08:22.340 --> 01:08:25.240
And I think a lot
of simplifications

01:08:25.240 --> 01:08:27.710
can happen-- because GPUs
were-- there's a lot of baggage,

01:08:27.710 --> 01:08:31.090
actually, if you think about--
because they were designed

01:08:31.090 --> 01:08:33.430
in an era where
you had to do a lot

01:08:33.430 --> 01:08:37.370
of branching and various
types of ad hoc computations

01:08:37.370 --> 01:08:41.510
which are not really needed
in the deep learning regime.

01:08:41.510 --> 01:08:43.660
So I think there are
quite a few opportunities

01:08:43.660 --> 01:08:47.410
to improve the hardware as well.

01:08:47.410 --> 01:08:50.649
I think there was a hand
back there, and I'll--

01:08:50.649 --> 01:08:53.359
I don't know if this is
like the right question

01:08:53.359 --> 01:08:57.710
but I'm thinking about, but
in the context of the lecture,

01:08:57.710 --> 01:09:02.560
it's basically a model that's
being trained in one go that's

01:09:02.560 --> 01:09:04.810
being optimized,
but I'm wondering

01:09:04.810 --> 01:09:07.000
if any of the techniques
that we're talking about

01:09:07.000 --> 01:09:09.920
can be used to incrementally
train a model--

01:09:09.920 --> 01:09:13.510
for example, as you
get new training data,

01:09:13.510 --> 01:09:17.330
not just to fine-tune, but
actually to recalculate

01:09:17.330 --> 01:09:19.560
everything without having
to recalculate everything.

01:09:19.560 --> 01:09:20.060
Yeah.

01:09:20.060 --> 01:09:22.850
So the question is,
can these techniques

01:09:22.850 --> 01:09:27.260
be used to essentially
do continued training?

01:09:27.260 --> 01:09:28.500
Yeah, absolutely.

01:09:28.500 --> 01:09:31.640
So if you think about the
unit of what we're working on,

01:09:31.640 --> 01:09:34.050
is just doing gradient steps.

01:09:34.050 --> 01:09:37.790
So if you take a
half-trained checkpoint,

01:09:37.790 --> 01:09:40.649
you can just continue
doing what this is.

01:09:40.649 --> 01:09:47.960
There's nothing specific about
starting from scratch here.

01:09:47.960 --> 01:09:49.580
I think there's
a question there.

01:09:49.580 --> 01:09:53.359
Yeah, so on the
model-specific hardware,

01:09:53.359 --> 01:09:57.770
[INAUDIBLE] previous
question, presumably

01:09:57.770 --> 01:10:00.440
there's a physical
technical reason

01:10:00.440 --> 01:10:06.440
why you can't make nodes much
larger than they are currently.

01:10:06.440 --> 01:10:13.310
What's the change that
you're talking about?

01:10:13.310 --> 01:10:18.160
So if you could
just make GPU nodes

01:10:18.160 --> 01:10:21.400
infinitely-- like as big as
you want, people would do that.

01:10:21.400 --> 01:10:24.450
So presumably there's a
tech-- like a hardware reason

01:10:24.450 --> 01:10:27.570
that's not possible, so what's
the actual advancement being

01:10:27.570 --> 01:10:31.200
done for the [INAUDIBLE]
specific hardware you mentioned?

01:10:31.200 --> 01:10:35.400
Yeah, so the question is there
are physical limits for sure

01:10:35.400 --> 01:10:38.880
for a GPU.

01:10:38.880 --> 01:10:40.090
Let me just go--

01:10:40.090 --> 01:10:46.910
so you can make GPUs obviously
infinitely large, or infinitely

01:10:46.910 --> 01:10:47.410
dense.

01:10:47.410 --> 01:10:51.720
I mean, there's
also power issues.

01:10:51.720 --> 01:10:54.760
You need to get rid
of all the heat.

01:10:54.760 --> 01:11:01.590
And, there's only so much
bandwidth that can fit.

01:11:01.590 --> 01:11:05.400
So I don't know the exact
details, but at least

01:11:05.400 --> 01:11:07.900
in some of the Cerebras case--

01:11:07.900 --> 01:11:14.960
I mean they have this way
of manufacturing basically

01:11:14.960 --> 01:11:21.540
the chips so that the
memory is on the chip.

01:11:21.540 --> 01:11:24.450
So I guess it's just a way
of putting it on there.

01:11:24.450 --> 01:11:28.400
And I think that
there are obviously

01:11:28.400 --> 01:11:32.690
trade-offs because it comes
at a cost of not having

01:11:32.690 --> 01:11:35.750
as much flexibility.

01:11:35.750 --> 01:11:39.710
But in general, I think
the way to maybe think

01:11:39.710 --> 01:11:44.030
about this more broadly
is that GPUs were still

01:11:44.030 --> 01:11:49.050
developing the CPU era where
it's much more control-focused.

01:11:49.050 --> 01:11:52.590
I have code that I'm executing,
that's the first-class citizen,

01:11:52.590 --> 01:11:59.610
and then data needs to be moved
to execute to handle the code.

01:11:59.610 --> 01:12:03.410
But the big difference with
deep learning workloads

01:12:03.410 --> 01:12:05.280
is that it's all data flow.

01:12:05.280 --> 01:12:08.750
The computation graph, if
you look at these, is static.

01:12:08.750 --> 01:12:12.530
You know from the beginning
exactly all the computations

01:12:12.530 --> 01:12:16.320
that are going to be done until
essentially the end of training.

01:12:16.320 --> 01:12:18.480
So using that
knowledge, you should

01:12:18.480 --> 01:12:22.350
be able to lay out your
computation in a much smarter

01:12:22.350 --> 01:12:25.980
way than having to deal
with the flexibility

01:12:25.980 --> 01:12:28.796
uncertainty over
ad hoc computation.

01:12:32.310 --> 01:12:33.070
OK.

01:12:33.070 --> 01:12:35.170
Maybe a few more questions
and I'll end there.

01:12:35.170 --> 01:12:35.670
Yeah?

01:12:35.670 --> 01:12:38.580
Is the computational graph
usually stored on the CPU

01:12:38.580 --> 01:12:40.470
or in the GPU?

01:12:40.470 --> 01:12:44.980
So the question is, where is
the computation graph stored?

01:12:44.980 --> 01:12:47.370
Well, the code is--

01:12:47.370 --> 01:12:53.880
I mean, all this code
is running on this CPU.

01:12:53.880 --> 01:13:01.998
But when you call something
like a PyTorch function

01:13:01.998 --> 01:13:05.490
that needs to run on GPU,
then it launches kernels

01:13:05.490 --> 01:13:09.950
under the hood, and the kernels
are code that runs on the GPU.

01:13:12.700 --> 01:13:15.040
Yeah, I'm not sure of that--

01:13:15.040 --> 01:13:18.670
so I guess maybe another answer
is that the computation graph is

01:13:18.670 --> 01:13:22.780
more of, I guess, a conceptual.

01:13:22.780 --> 01:13:27.100
It's not like there is a
graph literally that's being--

01:13:27.100 --> 01:13:33.250
I mean, I guess
there is, but it's

01:13:33.250 --> 01:13:36.750
not like the graph gets put on
the GPU, if that makes sense.

01:13:40.560 --> 01:13:41.060
OK.

01:13:41.060 --> 01:13:43.750
So these communication
primitives

01:13:43.750 --> 01:13:50.590
that [INAUDIBLE] can receive
and send [INAUDIBLE] CPU

01:13:50.590 --> 01:13:54.100
instructions [INAUDIBLE]

01:13:57.130 --> 01:13:59.480
So the question is, the
communication primitives,

01:13:59.480 --> 01:14:01.490
are they CPU or GPU?

01:14:01.490 --> 01:14:04.120
So these collective
operations are,

01:14:04.120 --> 01:14:08.230
in some sense,
abstract specification

01:14:08.230 --> 01:14:13.540
of what types of operations need
to happen, which can happen.

01:14:13.540 --> 01:14:18.030
If you remember, this
PyTorch distributed

01:14:18.030 --> 01:14:19.780
has different backends.

01:14:19.780 --> 01:14:25.124
So it could happen on a
GPU or it happen on a CPU.

01:14:25.124 --> 01:14:30.600
[INAUDIBLE] is it-- like,
is the CPU scheduling them

01:14:30.600 --> 01:14:34.800
or is it kernels
which are independent?

01:14:34.800 --> 01:14:35.760
Yeah.

01:14:35.760 --> 01:14:37.950
Well, the CPU drives--

01:14:37.950 --> 01:14:42.190
basically is the master still.

01:14:42.190 --> 01:14:45.040
And then when you do a
collective operation,

01:14:45.040 --> 01:14:48.130
it calls the NCCL
library, which launches,

01:14:48.130 --> 01:14:50.370
which is it's still
CPU, and then it

01:14:50.370 --> 01:14:54.840
launches some kernels
that move data around.

01:14:54.840 --> 01:14:55.610
Yeah.

01:14:55.610 --> 01:14:56.110
OK.

01:14:56.110 --> 01:14:57.870
Maybe this is a
good place to end.

01:14:57.870 --> 01:14:58.570
All right.

01:14:58.570 --> 01:15:00.900
I will see you next Monday--

01:15:00.900 --> 01:15:01.840
or Tuesday, rather.

01:15:04.560 --> 01:15:06.110
Thanks.
